{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zeszqenbqz4zcvlh4ljl42ejd6gek6swujw2dq7pmpuwe55ravaa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Nez6XuLzrv2CJ7CbTkc6RJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"tests/common/diff.rs\")\ns = p.read_text()\n\ns = s.replace('''/// The two libraries under comparison plus a reproducibility filter.\npub struct Pair {\n    pub c: Lib,\n    pub r: Lib,\n}\n\nimpl Pair {\n    pub fn new() -> Pair {\n        Pair {\n            c: super::c_lib(),\n            r: super::rust_lib(),\n        }\n    }\n    pub fn with_c(c: Lib) -> Pair {\n        Pair {\n            c,\n            r: super::rust_lib(),\n        }\n    }\n    pub fn reset(&self) {\n        self.c.reset_tables();\n        self.r.reset_tables();\n    }\n''','''/// Prefix that marks a case as \"not comparable\" rather than \"wrong\".\n/// `expect_no_mismatch` / `expect_all_pass` count these separately.\npub const SKIP: &str = \"SKIP:\";\n\npub fn skip(why: impl std::fmt::Display) -> Result<(), String> {\n    Err(format!(\"{SKIP} {why}\"))\n}\n\n/// The libraries under comparison.\n///\n/// `c` is the C source built `-O0 -DNDEBUG` -- identical code layout to the\n/// reference build, but with `assert()` compiled out, so it *returns* on\n/// malformed input instead of aborting. That is the behaviour `src/lib.rs`\n/// reproduces, and it is the strictest possible reference: every input where the\n/// C has a defined answer must match byte for byte.\n///\n/// `ca` is the reference build itself (`cmake`, no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so\n/// `assert()` is live). It is consulted only when `c` and `r` disagree: if the\n/// reference build `abort()`s on that input, the C's own `assert()`s say the\n/// input violated a documented precondition, so there is no defined result to\n/// agree with and the case is *skipped* rather than failed. Every skip is\n/// counted and reported