{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_463553gxzb5akijp44feeuv6dqoowrk3ss5oy4nerg52c526mvya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_018kzmxNR9XoZUmqTUV41iQi","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QGr4ci/translation/README.md","content":"# Rust translation of `c_src` (cute_png-derived PNG/DEFLATE decoder)\n\n`src/lib.rs` is a literal, ABI-compatible translation of `c_src/src/lib.c`\n(a trimmed derivative of `cute_png.h` by Randy Gaul; see `c_src/license.txt`).\nIt builds as a `cdylib` and exports exactly the same public symbols as the\nshared library produced by `c_src/CMakeLists.txt`.\n\n## Contents\n\n| file | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `src/lib.rs` | the translation |\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | Phase A.1 — the exported/imported symbol surface, both libraries |\n| `ERRORS.md` | Phase A.2 — every way the C rejects input, one row per branch |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | Phase A.3 — every runtime configuration the C branches on |\n| `build_diff.sh` | builds the reference C `.so`, four `NDEBUG` C `.so`s, and the Rust `.so` |\n| `run_all.sh` | runs the whole suite in every profile / feature combination |\n| `tests/` | the differential suite (see below) |\n\n## Exported ABI\n\n`nm -D -S --defined-only` yields the same nine symbols, with the same section\nclass and the same object sizes, in both libraries. `tests/symbols.rs` asserts\nthe diff is empty in *both* directions and that each exported table really is the\nstorage the decoder reads (a one-byte poke must change the decode).\n\n| symbol | type | size |\n|---|---|---|\n| `load_png_mem` | FUNC | — |\n| `cp_inflate` | FUNC | — |\n| `cp_error_reason` | OBJECT (`.bss`) | 8 |\n| `cp_fixed_table` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 320 |\n| `cp_permutation_order` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 19 |\n| `cp_len_extra_bits` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 31 |\n| `cp_len_base` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 124 |\n| `cp_dist_extra_bits` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 32 |\n| `cp_dist_base` | OBJECT (`.data`) | 128 |\n\nThere are no namespacing macros in `c_src/include/lib.h`, so linker names equal\nsource-level names and there are no macro-generated exports. The six tables and\n`cp_error_reason` are mutable globals in C and `static mut` here; the decoder\nreads them through raw pointers on every call, so a caller that mutates them\nchanges behaviour exactly as it does in C.\n\n`cp_image_t` (16 bytes: `int w; int h; cp_pixel_t *pix;`) is `#[repr(C)]` and\nreturned by value from `load_png_mem`, matching the SysV AMD64 register return\n(`RAX`/`RDX`). `cp_state_t` is `#[repr(C)]` and its layout is asserted against\nthe C compiler's (size 2464, `lookup` @72, `lit` @1096, `dst` @2248, `len`\n@2376). That is load-bearing, not cosmetic: `cp_decode` reads `tree[-1]` when a\nHuffman tree is empty, i.e. it reads the *preceding struct field*.\n\nMemory is allocated with libc `malloc`/`calloc` and released with libc `free`,\nso `img.pix` can be `free()`d by C code, and `cp_inflate`'s alignment-sensitive\n`first_bytes = ((in + 3) & ~3) - in` sees the same pointer alignments.\n\n## Fidelity notes\n\nBugs and quirks of the C are reproduced deliberately, including:\n\n* `w = cp_make32(ihdr) + 1` (the `+ 1` in the C source) and `img.w = w - 1`.\n* `cp_stored`'s `s->bits_left / 8 <= LEN` check, which rejects any stored\n  DEFLATE block that is not the last thing in the stream, and its `memcpy` that\n  ignores `out_end` (so a large `LEN` overruns the output buffer).\n* `cp_stored`'s **`cp_ptr` aliasing bug**: when the `final_word` path is taken\n  during the `LEN`/`NLEN` reads, `s->count` ends up 8 bits too high and\n  `cp_ptr()` returns a pointer 1..3 bytes *before* the payload, so a stored\n  block with fewer than 3 payload bytes decodes to the wrong data. Pinned down\n  byte-exactly in `valid_inflate.rs::stored_block_cp_ptr_quirk_for_short_payloads`.\n* `cp_unfilter`'s first row: filter 2 is a no-op, filter 4 uses\n  `cp_paeth(raw[x-bpp], 0, 0)`, and filters 1/3/4 start at `x = bpp`.\n* `cp_decode`'s `tree[lo - 1]` read when `hi == 0` — `s->lit[-1]` is the tail of\n  `s->lookup` and `s->dst[-1]` *is* `s->lit[287]`.\n* Signed vs unsigned pointer arithmetic: `cp_chunk` uses `int offset = len + 12`\n  (sign-extended, so a chunk length >= 0x7FFFFFF4 walks *backwards* and passes\n  the bound test), while `cp_find` uses `png->p += len + 12` in `uint32_t` (so a\n  length of `0xFFFFFFF4` wraps the stride to 0 and loops for ever — reproduced,\n  see `errors.rs::e9b_chunk_length_that_wraps_the_cp_find_stride_hangs_both`).\n* `cp_find`'s shared cursor: a `PLTE` placed *after* the IDATs is still found,\n  and consuming it moves the cursor past the image data\n  (`errors.rs::e31b_palette_after_the_idats_is_found_and_consumes_the_cursor`).\n* All integer truncation/wrap-around (`datalen += len` through `uint32`,\n  `pix_bytes` truncated to `int`, `cp_make32` shifting into the sign bit, the\n  *unsigned* `(int64_t)w*h*sizeof(cp_pixel_t) < INT_MAX` comparison, ...).\n* Variable shift counts above the operand width are masked to 6 bits, matching\n  what x86-64 `shl`/`shr` do for the C's undefined cases.\n* The exact order of every validation check and the exact error strings,\n  including the misspelled `\"innapropriate window size detected\"`.\n\n### The `.data` layout of the exported tables\n\n`cp_block` indexes `cp_len_extra_bits`/`cp_len_base` with `symbol - 257` and the\n`cp_dist_*` tables with a distance symbol, neither bounds-checked. A corrupt\nHuffman tree makes `cp_decode` return a symbol far outside those tables, so the\nC reads *past* the end of one table into whatever the linker put next.\n\nIn the reference library (`cmake`, no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, i.e. `-O0`) those six\ntables are the entire `.data` section — 672 bytes, each table 32-byte aligned,\nin **source order**, gap bytes zero:\n\n```\n   0  cp_fixed_table (320)\n 320  cp_permutation_order (19)  + 13 gap\n 352  cp_len_extra_bits (31)     +  1 gap\n 384  cp_len_base (124)          +  4 gap\n 512  cp_dist_extra_bits (32)\n 544  cp_dist_base (128)                    == .data size 0x2a0\n```\n\nRust/LLVM orders and aligns statics differently, and that order cannot be\ncontrolled portably, so the four indexed reads resolve their index through a\nmodel of the layout above (`blob_byte`) instead of walking off the end of a Rust\nstatic. The model reads the *live* statics, so a caller that mutates a table\nstill affects out-of-range reads just as in C. `tests/blob_layout.rs` checks the\nmodel against the loaded reference library byte for byte.\n\nTwo caveats, both verified rather than assumed:\n\n* **Optimisation level changes the order.** At `-O2`/`-O3`/`-Os` gcc emits the\n  six tables in *reverse* source order. The model targets the build the task\n  specifies (`-O0`); nothing can match both.\n* **Past `.data` there is nothing to match.** Offsets >= 672 are outside every\n  object the library owns: 672..688 is `.bss` (`completed.0`, then the\n  ASLR-randomised `cp_error_reason` *pointer*), and from 688 on the last\n  partially-used page still holds the bytes that follow `.data` in the ELF\n  *file* — the `.comment` string and the section headers. The model returns 0\n  there. `blob_layout.rs::reads_past_the_data_section_are_not_a_property_of_the_program`\n  demonstrates that the reference C library gives different answers for the same\n  read depending only on how it was compiled.\n  Reachability: the only route to an out-of-range table index is `s->dst[-1]`\n  (an empty distance tree), and `cp_dist_base` is the *last* table, so a distance\n  symbol >= 32 always lands outside `.data`. Distance symbols 0..31 are fully\n  in range and are tested exhaustively\n  (`valid_inflate.rs::c24_empty_distance_tree_reads_lit_287`).\n\n### `assert()` and the reference build\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so the library the task's\nbuild command produces has **`assert()` live** and `abort()`s on malformed\ninput. The translation reproduces the `NDEBUG` behaviour instead — what every\n`-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release/RelWithDebInfo/MinSizeRel` build does, and the only\noption for a library that must not kill its caller.\n\n`ERRORS.md` has a row per assert. `tests/asserts.rs` proves, for all ten, that\neach is either reached by a concrete input (identified by name from the C's own\n`__assert_fail` message, captured off the child's `stderr`) or unreachable with\nan argument — and that on every one of those inputs the Rust agrees byte for byte\nwith the same C source compiled `-O0 -DNDEBUG`. In other words the *only*\ndifference from the reference build is the `abort()` itself.\n\n### Deliberate deviations (all of them C undefined behaviour)\n\nFour local arrays are padded / zero-initialised so malformed input cannot cause\nRust UB. Every one is a place where the C reads or writes outside an object.\n\n1. **`cp_build`: `int codes[16], first[16], counts[16]` -> 256 entries each,\n   zero-initialised.** Reachable when a code length exceeds 15, which the C\n   catches with `assert(len < 16)`; under `NDEBUG` it indexes past `counts` into\n   `first`/`codes` and then uses *uninitialised* `first[len]`/`codes[len]`.\n   Guarded by the C's own assert (row A9).\n2. **`cp_dynamic`: `uint8_t lens[288 + 32]` -> `[288 + 32 + 256]`.** The 16/17/18\n   run-length symbols can push the write index up to 137 entries past the end of\n   the C array. In the reference `-O0` frame `lens` is at `rbp-0x180` and the\n   overrun runs straight through `lenlens`, `sym`, `nlen`, `ndst`, `nlit`, the\n   `i` counters, **`n` (the loop counter, at `lens[376..380]`)**, the saved `rbp`\n   and the return address. Zeroing `n` restarts the loop for ever, and the\n   reference build does hang — while at `-O2`/`-Os` the same input segfaults and\n   at `-O3` it returns an error.\n   `undefined_behaviour.rs::cp_dynamic_stack_overrun_hangs_the_c_but_not_the_rust`\n   demonstrates all four outcomes from the one input.\n3. **`cp_dynamic`: `lens[n - 1]` at `n == 0`** reads an indeterminate value in C\n   (the stack byte below an uninitialised array); 0 is used here.\n4. **`cp_dynamic`: `uint8_t lenlens[19]` -> 256 entries.** Only reachable if a\n   caller writes a value >= 19 into the exported `cp_permutation_order`, since\n   the C bounds the *index* into that table but not the *value* it finds.\n   In the reference `-O0` frame `lenlens` is at `rbp-0x40`, so a value of 36\n   lands on `ndst` and a value of 60 on the loop counter `i`;\n   `undefined_behaviour.rs::mutated_permutation_order_overruns_lenlens_differently_per_compiler`\n   shows the reference build and the `-O2`/`-O3`/`-Os` builds disagreeing on the\n   same input. `errors.rs::g14a_...` requires exact agreement for every in-range\n   table mutation; `g14b_...` skips exactly these two out-of-bounds cases, from\n   the *mutation* rather than from the outcome.\n\nOut-of-bounds *reads* that the C performs on real objects (past the end of a\n`PLTE` chunk, past the input buffer, `tree[-1]`, table lookups driven by a\ncorrupt Huffman tree) are reproduced with raw pointers rather than \"fixed\".\n\n## Verification\n\n```sh\n./build_diff.sh     # C reference .so + four NDEBUG C .so + the Rust .so\n./run_all.sh        # every suite, every profile, every feature combination\n```\n\nBoth libraries are loaded with `libloading` and driven **only** through their\nexported symbols, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are part of what is tested. For\neach case the harness compares the return value, `cp_error_reason`, `img.w`,\n`img.h`, `img.pix == NULL`, and every byte of the output — for `cp_inflate` the\n*whole* output allocation including 256 bytes of slack past `out_bytes`, so an\noverrun is caught rather than ignored.\n\n| suite | what it covers |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/smoke.rs` | the harness itself, plus a real 4x3 PNG decoded to known pixels |\n| `tests/symbols.rs` | Phase D: `nm -D` parity, `SYMBOLS.md` freshness, no unresolved imports, exported tables are live storage |\n| `tests/blob_layout.rs` | the `.data` model vs the loaded C library |\n| `tests/valid_inflate.rs` | Phase B, rows C1–C24 (`cp_inflate` driven directly) |\n| `tests/valid_png.rs` | Phase B, rows C25–C51 (`load_png_mem` end to end) |\n| `tests/errors.rs` | Phase C, rows E1–E31 and G1–G14 |\n| `tests/asserts.rs` | Phase C, rows A1–A10 |\n| `tests/fuzz.rs` | ~14 000 mutated / structured-random inputs, plus anti-vacuity tallies |\n| `tests/corpus_coverage.rs` | proves the corpora are not vacuous and that the harness can fail |\n| `tests/undefined_behaviour.rs` | the two deviations above, with the C disagreeing with itself |\n\nInputs on which the reference build trips one of its own `assert()`s, or on which\nthe C walks outside an object, are **skipped explicitly and counted**; a suite\nfails if skips ever swallow the batch, so a green run cannot be vacuous.\nEvery case runs inside a forked child with a watchdog, so a C crash or hang is\nattributed to the exact input and the rest of the batch still runs.\n\nCoverage evidence from the fuzz tallies: 17 of the 18 distinct `load_png_mem`\nrejection strings are reached by mutation alone (the remaining two —\n`\"unable to allocate raw image space\"` and `\"invalid image size found\"` — have\ndedicated tests; the latter is proved unreachable), alongside 915 successful\ndecodes out of 4 000 mutations.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4264,"cache_read_input_tokens":452308,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4264,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca950163-4526-4c83-bcdb-90a4f8c566ab","uuid":"7ec302d6-3253-4363-bcdd-ca42eebad364","timestamp":"2026-08-27T14:59:31.511Z"}
